  "connector": "tidak / bukan",
  "gloss": "not",
  "title": "tidak and bukan — not",
  "pattern": "tidak + verb or describing word · bukan + noun · …, bukan?",
  "explanation": "Indonesian has two words for 'not', and the word after it decides which. Tidak goes before verbs and describing words: Saya tidak makan daging (I don't eat meat), Ini tidak pedas (This isn't spicy). Bukan goes before nouns, to say what something isn't: Saya bukan guru (I'm not a teacher), Ini bukan kopi (This isn't coffee). In casual speech tidak becomes nggak, but bukan stays bukan.",
  "natural": "Bukan on the end turns a sentence into a check, like 'isn't it?' or 'right?': Ini hotelnya, bukan? (This is the hotel, isn't it?). In speech that tag is usually cut to kan, or swapped for a soft ya.",
  "stiff": "Tidak before a noun is the classic mistake: Saya tidak guru is wrong; it's Saya bukan guru. Nggak is for friends: with an older stranger or at an office, say tidak.",
